];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens use tools in the wild?', a: 'Documented tool use by wild Common Ravens is rare and limited. Unlike New Caledonian crows, ravens do not routinely manufacture or deploy tools for foraging. What ravens do show is extensive object manipulation — using their bill to lever, pry, and probe in ways that parallel tool use. In captivity, ravens have solved multi-step tool-use tasks (pulling string to retrieve food, using stones to break open hard food items), suggesting the cognitive capacity exists but is rarely deployed in wild conditions where it is not necessary.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens better at using tools than crows?', a: 'No — crows (particularly New Caledonian crows, Corvus moneduloides) are significantly more consistent tool users than ravens. New Caledonian crows manufacture hooked tools from leaves and twigs to extract insects from bark, and wild juveniles learn tool-use techniques through social transmission. Common Ravens have greater cognitive flexibility in social and future-planning tasks, but their physical tool use is less developed. Both species are significantly more capable than most birds, but in different cognitive domains.' },
  { q: 'What objects do ravens manipulate?', a: 'Ravens manipulate a wide range of objects in both play and apparent problem-solving: sticks, stones, pieces of metal, food containers, and novel objects encountered in their environment. Object manipulation in ravens is closely linked to play behaviour — ravens will carry, drop, and fiddle with objects for extended periods in what appears to be exploration. Some researchers argue that object play in ravens may be the foundation from which tool-use cognition could develop, even if it has not resulted in systematic tool use in this species.' },
];
